A||||6||Uaana||Strut replacement||||1||
Z||000000||Uaana||05-27-2001||03:55 AM||||Someone help before I kill myself accidently.<BR>I have new kyb's in the mail as you read.<BR>My question is after reading the bible of auto repair "Haynes" <BR>There is no mention of using a spring compressor while replacing the struts. hell they make it sound like replacing a set of shocks.<BR>Did I miss something or is it really this easy?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Coop88||05-27-2001||05:47 AM||||Haven't replaced the struts on the Fiero yet, but on my other car (chevy celebrity) I needed spring compressors. Someone correct me if I'm wrong, but I'm pretty sure the springs are under tension, and a spring compressor would be needed. It's really not hard. You can rent them at Autozone. I think $40-50. You get your money back when you return them. Better safe than sorry||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||05-27-2001||06:46 AM||||Definitley need a spring compressor.  The top nut was the only pain that I ran into while changing struts.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL><BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pictures-1_5.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steve Normington||05-27-2001||10:20 AM||||Get some lowering springs (JC Whitney). Then you don't need spring compressors.<P>Checker also loans tools for free. You just give them a deposit equal to the price.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||05-27-2001||11:07 AM||||The stock springs need compressors. The lowering springs don't unless they are a very minor lowering.<P>If you can't move the spring in the perches, use compressor springs. Otherwise, you are taking your life into your own hands.||||reg||4
Z||000005||sjp777||05-27-2001||11:20 AM||||You'll still need the compressor to get the old ones off. Even when I was putting new lowering springs on I had to compress them a little, just not as much as the old ones coming off. You can rent the compressor from an auto parts store for about $5.<P>Changing springs is the easy part, getting the old struts off can be a bear. Those two big bolts holding the bottoms on get exposed to a lot of moisture. They can be most un-cooperative. Better start spraying them with WD40 now, and again everyday until you get your new struts. Have a big breaker bar handy.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">    <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||05-27-2001||01:27 PM||||The strut assembly is held by 3 nuts up top and 2 big bolts on bottom. Pull the whole thing out to work on it.<P>Should you need a big breaker on the 2 big bolts.... jack/block under the suspension to keep it from bouncing. Be carfull you don't block against the greese fitting on the lower ball joint.<P>Easiest way.... take the whole strut assembly to a shop and let them change the springs and stuff to the new strut. Allot of parts shops have air/hyrdraulic compressors. takes minutes and there's no danger to you... Cost a few bucks but makes life real easy...<P>put some new boots on the strut shafts to protect them... If you look around you can get boots in diferant colors. The boots keep the strut shaft from getting whacked by rocks and things. Also helps keep dirt off the strut top seal.<P>The top cap has a bearing in it... make sure it's not gone sloppy. Even tho Fiero doesn't make much use of this bearing, a sloppy one can effect handling.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-27-2001).]||||reg||1
